investigators believe they might be a  step closer to understanding the motives  of Germanwings copilot andreas Lubitz  they uncovered new evidence from a  tablet computer found in his apartment  in Dusseldorf the browser fell off  vanished crushed the search history in  the browser had not been deleted we  analyzed the search terms used from the  16th to the 23rd of march according to  our results the user looked for  information about therapy as well as  about different forms of suicide on at  least one day he also spent several  minutes searching for information about  cockpit doors in particular on how they  are secured tuned wouldn't even see  shades for here when I was an undergrad  these latest findings leave little room  for doubt about what happened it seems  the 27 year old copilot took advantage  of his colleagues absence from the  cockpit and deliberately crashed the  Germanwings plane into the rocky ravine  taking 149 people with him to their  deaths meanwhile there were also  developments from French investigators  today the second flight recorder was  found by recovery workers at the crash  site this after more than a week of  intensive search the flight data  recorder records the route speed and  altitude of the plane should the data be  usable and analysis could give further  insight into the crash the cockpit voice  recorder was recovered one day after the  accident letting investigators here the  last conversations and noises from the  flight deck that box provided the first  evidence to imply that the copilot could  have deliberately brought the Airbus  down nine days later investigators now  think they might have a clearer picture  of why this tragedy occurred
 

